# This machine's own settings for the LocalLLM scripts: ~/.config/localllm/local.env (mode 600). Reference listing
# every setting: on a fresh install, restore.sh (run by install.sh) puts local.env back from the backup (install/pi-mcp.sh
# only creates one from this template if run standalone without a backup). It stays out of the repo, so where your backups and passphrase are isn't in it; the settings backup saves
# it (the encrypted archive). install/pi-mcp.sh adds PI_MCP_ADAPTER_OAUTH_FILE_KEY to it by itself: the key of Pi's
# encrypted Context7 sign-in; keep that line. The backup passphrase itself never goes in here.

# The file holding the backup passphrase (one line, mode 600). archive-backup.sh and the scheduled backup read it;
# without it, archive-backup.sh asks for the passphrase and the scheduled backup can't run.
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE="$HOME/path/to/your/passphrase-file"

# Where archive-backup.sh keeps its encrypted archives (the newest BACKUP_KEEP_COUNT).
# BACKUP_ARCHIVE_DIR="$HOME/path/to/archive-folder"

# Where upload-backup.sh (and the scheduled backup) uploads them: an rclone remote and a folder in it, as
# remote:folder. Create the remote with `rclone config` first.
# BACKUP_REMOTE="your-remote:your-folder"

# How many encrypted archives to keep, in the archive folder and on the remote: the newest ones stay, older ones are
# deleted after each new backup. With the scheduled backup every 6 hours, 8 is about 2 days of PC use.
# BACKUP_KEEP_COUNT="8"

# The model: a profile in the repo's models/ folder (file name without .env); unset, the one named in models/default.
# After changing it: install/llama.sh (downloads it), install/pi.sh, then stop/llama.sh && start/llama.sh.
# MODEL_PROFILE="name-of-a-profile"

# llama-server, tuned to this PC's GPU and CPU (README, "Tuning llama-server"). Each is optional:
# - LLAMA_CUDA_ARCH: the GPU generation install/llama.sh builds for (89 = RTX 40, 86 = RTX 30, 75 = RTX 20,
#   61 = GTX 10); unset, it's read from the GPU (nvidia-smi). A change rebuilds llama.cpp on the next install/llama.sh.
# - LLAMA_N_CPU_MOE: the model's layers whose experts stay on the CPU (out of the profile's MOE_LAYERS); unset, the
#   profile's N_CPU_MOE (33 of 40 for the default profile, which fits an 8 GB GPU). Less VRAM: raise it; more VRAM:
#   lower it. Ignored for models without experts.
# - LLAMA_THREADS: CPU threads; unset, the number of physical cores.
# - LLAMA_CACHE_RAM_MIB: RAM for the prompt cache, in MiB; unset, the profile's CACHE_RAM_MIB.
# - LLAMA_VISION: off leaves the vision projector out (no image input, about 0.9 GB of RAM freed); unset, on.
# After changing them: stop/llama.sh && start/llama.sh.
# LLAMA_CUDA_ARCH="89"
# LLAMA_N_CPU_MOE="33"
# LLAMA_THREADS="8"
# LLAMA_CACHE_RAM_MIB="2048"
# LLAMA_VISION="on"

# GPU limits for long model runs (less heat and fan noise, a little slower), set by services/gpu-limits.sh each
# time llama-server starts. Unset, the GPU's defaults are put back.
# - GPU_MAX_CLOCK_MHZ: caps the GPU's clock; laptop GPUs allow it. Text generation is limited mostly by memory
#   speed, so a cap of about 70% of the GPU's top clock (nvidia-smi -q -d CLOCK, "Max Clocks") costs little speed.
# - GPU_POWER_LIMIT_W: a power limit in watts; most laptop GPUs refuse it (the driver manages their power).
# After changing them: stop/llama.sh && start/llama.sh (a change to the service itself: install/llama.sh).
# GPU_MAX_CLOCK_MHZ="2100"
# GPU_POWER_LIMIT_W="80"

# Pi's subagents (code-finder, web-researcher, planner, worker, reviewer): how many may run at once. 1 (also when unset) runs them one after
# another, the right choice for a PC with one mid-range GPU. More runs them side by side, each in its own llama-server
# slot, so it's capped at the profile's SLOTS (and each one shares the GPU). After changing it: install/pi-config.sh
# (writes model.json), then restart Pi.
# SUBAGENTS_AT_ONCE="1"
# The most subagents one message of yours may start, in all (8 when unset). The model is told the limit when it asks
# for more. Not capped at the slots: past SUBAGENTS_AT_ONCE they wait their turn.
# SUBAGENTS_MAX="4"

# Optional: your own browser setup (the repo stays browser-agnostic):
# - AGENT_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE_PATH: custom Chromium-based browser executable for agent-browser (unset: its own Chromium).
#   After changing it: install/pi-packages.sh (updates ~/.agent-browser/config.json).
# - AGENT_BROWSER_EXTENSIONS: bash array of unpacked browser extension folders for agent-browser to load (unset: none).
#   backup.sh saves folders under $HOME automatically and restore.sh puts them back. After changing it:
#   install/pi-packages.sh (updates ~/.agent-browser/config.json).
# - AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS: extra Chromium launch flags for agent-browser (comma- or newline-separated string; unset: none).
#   Use "--headless=new" to keep Chromium headless even when extensions are loaded. After changing it:
#   install/pi-packages.sh (updates ~/.agent-browser/config.json).
# - BROWSER_INSTALLER: script install.sh runs (with --update when passed) to install/update your browser; put its path
#   in EXTRA_BACKUP_FILES so backups keep it. Unset: install.sh doesn't install a browser.
# - BROWSER_POLICY_DIR: Chromium-style policy directory (e.g. /etc/chromium/policies, /etc/opt/chrome/policies) where
#   install/browser-address.sh writes the Open WebUI autoplay policy and install/memory.sh writes Memory Saver.
#   Unset: no browser policies are written.
# AGENT_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE_PATH="/path/to/your-browser"
# AGENT_BROWSER_EXTENSIONS=(
#     "$HOME/path/to/unpacked-extension"
# )
# AGENT_BROWSER_ARGS="--headless=new"
# BROWSER_INSTALLER="$HOME/.config/localllm/install-browser.sh"
# BROWSER_POLICY_DIR="/etc/chromium/policies"

# Extra files of your own to keep in the settings backup (anywhere under your home folder), as a bash array; restore.sh
# puts them back at the same place. For example notes or plans that live next to this setup.
# EXTRA_BACKUP_FILES=(
#     "$HOME/path/to/a/file"
# )

# Packages this PC needs that the setup itself doesn't, as a bash array: install.sh installs them with pacman. For
# example your laptop vendor's fan tool, for tools/fans/.
# EXTRA_PACKAGES=(
#     "your-fan-tool"
# )

# tools/fans/balanced.sh and performance.sh: the commands that switch the laptop's fan profile with your laptop
# vendor's tool (its package in EXTRA_PACKAGES). Unset, the scripts say so and change nothing.
# FAN_PROFILE_BALANCED="your-fan-tool profile balanced"
# FAN_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE="your-fan-tool profile performance"

# The name check in tests/guard-tests.mjs: words that must never appear in the repo's files or commit messages,
# besides the values above (where the backups go, the helper's names, the packages and fan commands) and this PC's
# user, host, Tailscale and model names, which it checks by itself. For example the brands and models of your PCs,
# your storage provider, people's names. Whole words, any case.
# NAME_CHECK_WORDS=(
#     "your-laptop-brand"
#     "your-name"
# )

# The two-PC setup (README, "Two-PC setup"): single (everything on this PC, also when unset) or split (Open WebUI, the
# search gateway, Kokoro and the voice models on a helper PC; llama-server, Pi and SearXNG stay here). Change it with
# ./localllm.sh switch single|split, which also moves the services and Open WebUI's data; not by editing it here.
# LOCALLLM_MODE="single"
# Which apps run: pi (Pi only: Open WebUI and its exclusive tools stay off, and llama-server gives all of its
# context to Pi's single slot) or pi,owui (default when unset: Pi + Open WebUI). Change it with
# ./localllm.sh apps pi|pi,owui, which applies it right away.
# LOCALLLM_APPS="pi,owui"
# The helper PC's Tailscale name and its Windows user name: ./localllm.sh setup-helper asks for both and writes them.
# HELPER_HOST="your-helper.your-tailnet.ts.net"
# HELPER_USER="your-windows-user"
# This PC's Tailscale name, as the helper reaches it; unset, read from `tailscale status`.
# MAIN_HOST="your-main-pc.your-tailnet.ts.net"
# Split mode only: run a service on the other PC than config/services.json says (main or helper); it takes the one it
# works with along (the voice router with Kokoro, Open WebUI with the search gateway). E.g. Kokoro on this PC:
# PLACE_KOKORO="main"
